Images and containers|An image is a packaged filesystem and configuration; a container is a running or stopped instance created from it.|Containers add a writable layer and runtime settings such as mounts, limits and networks. Replacing a container does not change its source image. Persist required data outside the disposable writable layer.|docker ps -a
Layering|Image layers record filesystem changes and can be shared between images.|A later layer can hide a file without removing it from an earlier layer. Copying a secret and deleting it in a later instruction can leave the secret recoverable. Layer reuse reduces transfer and storage when content matches.|docker image history IMAGE
Dockerfiles|A Dockerfile describes build steps and default image metadata.|FROM selects a base, RUN executes a build step, COPY adds files, and CMD or ENTRYPOINT defines startup behavior. Build-time and runtime environments differ. Pin dependencies and minimize unnecessary packages to make builds more predictable.|
Build context|The build context is the set of files available to a build.|COPY paths are resolved within that context, and .dockerignore excludes unnecessary or sensitive files. A large context slows transfer and increases accidental disclosure risk. Keep credentials out of it and choose the intended context directory explicitly.|docker build --check .
Container logs|Container logs expose output captured from the container's configured logging path, commonly stdout and stderr.|Application logs written only inside the filesystem may not appear in docker logs. Logging drivers and retention determine availability. Correlate timestamps with restart and deployment history instead of assuming the latest lines describe the failure.|docker logs --tail 100 CONTAINER
Port publishing|Publishing maps a host address and port to a container port.|The application must still listen on the correct address inside the container. EXPOSE documents a port but does not publish it. Binding a published port to loopback can restrict host-side reachability compared with all interfaces.|docker port CONTAINER
Volumes|A Docker volume stores data independently of a particular container's writable layer.|Mounting a volume at a path can obscure image content at that location. Container replacement preserves the volume unless it is separately removed. Backup consistency and ownership still depend on the application and filesystem.|docker volume inspect VOLUME
Entrypoints|ENTRYPOINT and CMD define how a container starts and which default arguments it receives.|Exec-form startup passes signals more directly than a shell wrapper that does not exec its child. Runtime arguments can replace CMD or interact with ENTRYPOINT. Inspect the effective command when a container exits immediately.|docker inspect CONTAINER
PID one|The first process in a container's PID namespace has init-like responsibilities.|It must handle termination appropriately and reap exited children it owns. An application or wrapper that ignores signals can delay shutdown until forced termination. A small init process can help with forwarding and reaping where needed.|
Health checks|A container health check runs a configured probe to report application health separately from process existence.|A process may be alive while its service is unusable. Probe interval, timeout and startup behavior affect detection. A Docker health status alone does not guarantee an automatic restart; the surrounding orchestration policy matters.|docker inspect --format '{{json .State.Health}}' CONTAINER
User identity|Container processes run with numeric user and group identities that influence file and kernel permissions.|An image USER instruction sets a default, but runtime configuration can override it. Host-mounted files retain host ownership semantics. Running as non-root reduces privilege but does not eliminate kernel or mount-related risks.|docker inspect --format '{{.Config.User}}' CONTAINER
Memory limits|A container memory limit constrains memory through kernel resource controls.|The application may be killed when its cgroup cannot satisfy demand even if the host has free memory. Compare the limit with peak working set, cache and concurrency. Increasing the limit without understanding growth can move the failure to the host.|docker stats --no-stream
CPU quotas|CPU quotas limit how much CPU time a container group can consume during a period.|Quota exhaustion can throttle runnable work and increase tail latency. CPU shares instead influence relative scheduling weight under contention. Inspect throttling and demand rather than interpreting an idle host as proof of adequate container CPU.|
Bind mounts|A bind mount exposes a specific host path inside a container.|Changes may affect the host directly, and host path availability and ownership become deployment dependencies. A mount can obscure files packaged in the image. Use read-only mounts when writes are unnecessary and verify source paths carefully.|docker inspect --format '{{json .Mounts}}' CONTAINER
Read-only filesystems|A read-only root filesystem prevents writes to the container's root filesystem layer.|Applications may still need explicitly writable paths for temporary files, sockets or durable data. Mount only those paths with appropriate storage. Read-only root does not make separately mounted volumes read-only.|
Multi-stage builds|Multi-stage builds use several build stages and copy selected outputs into a final image.|Compilers and build dependencies can remain in earlier stages while runtime artifacts go into a smaller final image. Copy only required files and verify runtime libraries are present. A smaller image is useful but not automatically secure.|
Build cache|The build cache reuses results when relevant build inputs have not changed.|Instruction ordering affects how much work must be repeated. Copy dependency manifests before frequently changing source where appropriate. A cached step may also preserve old package results, so dependency pinning and intentional refresh policy matter.|
Secrets in builds|Build secrets provide temporary sensitive inputs without intentionally baking them into image layers.|Use the builder's secret mechanism rather than ARG, ENV or COPY for credentials. A build command can still leak a secret into output files or logs. Inspect resulting artifacts and keep secret use narrowly scoped.|
Image digests|An image digest identifies content, while a tag is a mutable name pointing to an image.|Deploying by digest improves reproducibility and supports provenance checks. A multi-platform image index and a platform-specific manifest have different digests. Record what was actually deployed and use an update process for security fixes.|docker image inspect IMAGE
Networks|Docker networks connect containers and provide scoped addressing and name resolution according to the network driver.|User-defined bridge networks support container-name resolution. Containers on different networks are not automatically mutually reachable. Host publishing and inter-container communication are separate paths with different controls.|docker network inspect NETWORK
Compose dependencies|Compose defines related services, networks and volumes for an application.|Dependency ordering alone does not guarantee application readiness. Health-based dependency conditions can help initial startup, while applications still need runtime retries and failure handling. Inspect the rendered configuration after variable substitution.|docker compose config
Restart policy|A restart policy determines when the runtime restarts a container after it exits.|Restarting can recover transient failures but can also create a tight crash loop. It does not repair invalid configuration, missing data or an unhealthy process that never exits. Coordinate restart behavior with graceful shutdown and external orchestration.|
Registry access|A registry stores and distributes image manifests and layers.|Pull failures can arise from authentication, authorization, missing tags, network errors or rate limits. Verify the exact image reference and target platform before rebuilding. Keep registry credentials scoped and avoid embedding them in images.|
Resource cleanup|Cleanup removes unused containers, images, build cache or volumes to reclaim space.|Each resource type has different ownership and data-loss implications. Inspect usage and references before pruning; an unused volume may still hold valuable data. Avoid broad automated pruning without a documented retention policy.|docker system df
Socket exposure|The Docker daemon socket exposes a control API with powerful host-level effects.|A process with access can often create privileged containers or mount host paths. Mounting the socket into an untrusted workload defeats many container boundaries. Treat daemon API access as privileged infrastructure access.|
Rootless mode|Rootless Docker runs the daemon and containers without a root-owned daemon using user-namespace mechanisms.|It reduces some host privilege exposure but has networking, storage and resource-control requirements. Check supported features for the environment. Rootless operation does not remove the need for image hygiene, least privilege and patching.|docker info
Image scanning|Image scanning identifies known vulnerabilities or policy findings in image contents.|Results depend on package detection, advisory data and the actual deployed digest. Triage exploitability and exposure while maintaining update discipline. A clean scan does not prove the application is free of unknown vulnerabilities or unsafe configuration.|
Supply chain|Container supply-chain controls establish where an image came from and how it was built.|Pin inputs, record provenance, verify signatures according to policy and retain an SBOM where useful. Build and deploy identities need constrained permissions. Verification must cover the digest actually deployed, not merely a similarly named tag.|
Shared kernel|Containers typically share the host kernel while isolating selected resource views.|A kernel defect can affect multiple containers and the host. Namespaces, cgroups, seccomp and privilege restrictions provide different layers of protection. Containers are not equivalent to independent virtual-machine kernels.|
Stateful migration|Stateful container migration moves an application together with compatible durable data.|Plan data consistency, schema compatibility, ownership and downtime before replacing containers. Copying a live data directory may not yield a valid database backup. Test restore and rollback with the application's supported procedures.|
